     "derivation": "Two problems. First the form: Arabic نَرْد nard is the backgammon word (a clipping of نَرْدَشِير nardašīr, from Persian nard / MP nēw-Ardaxšīr), not the plant; the Arabic names of spikenard are نَارْدِين nārdīn / نَارَدِين nāradīn and, by metathesis, رَنْد rand. Nourai's own reference PLA:257 in fact prints نارْدين, not نرد: Asbaghi, Persische Lehnwörter im Arabischen p.257, 'nārdīn: „Spickenard, Lavendel“ pers. ← skr. nard, mand. nard, syr. …, hebr. נרד'. Second the direction: Asbaghi does count nārdīn as a Persian loan in Arabic, but Wiktionary (with the Comprehensive Aramaic Lexicon and Löw) derives Arabic نَارْدِين directly 'from Aramaic נָרְדִּין / ܢܪܕܝܢ (nārdīn), a plurale tantum form of נָרְדָּא / ܢܪܕܐ (nārdā)', and Arabic رَنْد is 'alleged to be a metathesis of what is found as Hebrew נֵרְדְּ, Aramaic נִרְדָּא, Akkadian lardu, Ancient Greek νάρδος, Sanskrit नलद, and Arabic نَارْدِين'. Dehkhodā (quoting Aqrab al-Mawārid) and Moʿīn (Borhān p.2093 n.) treat the Arabic form as an Arabicization of the Greek. Above all, the node cannot bear the weight the chart puts on it: it is drawn as the source of Greek nardos, which is chronologically impossible (see node #6). So: the plant word did travel westwards through Semitic, but through Aramaic/Phoenician-Hebrew rather than Arabic, and the Arabic form is nārdīn/rand, not nard.",
