     "derivation": "Greek νάρδος cannot come from Arabic. The Greek word is in use by the 4th century BCE (Theophrastus, Hist. plant.; then Dioscorides, Mark 14:3) and the Septuagint uses it for Hebrew נֵרְדְּ nērd in the Song of Songs — roughly a thousand years before Arabic is a written language with any influence on Greek, and long before any Arab trade in the Aegean. Every modern authority routes it through north-west Semitic instead: Beekes (Etymological Dictionary of Greek, 2010, p.996), followed by Wiktionary, has νάρδος 'from Phoenician (compare Hebrew נֵרְדְּ nērd, Aramaic נִרְדָּא nirdā, Akkadian lardu, Arabic رَنْد rand, Old South Arabian rnd), possibly from Sanskrit नलद nálada (although a Semitic-to-Indian loan has also been suggested)'; Klein has 'fr. Gk. νάρδος, fr. Heb. nērdh'; Etymonline 'from Greek nardos, a word borrowed from a Semitic language (compare Hebrew ner'd … Arabic and Persian nardin), ultimately from Sanskrit narada, nalada'; Skeat, whom Nourai cites on node #4, jumps straight 'Gk. νάρδος — Pers. nard — Skt. nalada' with no Semitic or Arabic stage at all. So the node's word, language and semantics are right, but its parent should be Phoenician/Hebrew-Aramaic (or, on Skeat's and Horn's older account, Iranian directly), not Arabic. Note also that Arabic رند rand and نارْدين nārdīn are, on Beekes's showing, sisters of Greek νάρδος within the Semitic group, not its ancestors.",
